{"message":"I followed the required review steps (I reviewed the ticket context, the collaboration note, and the security guidance) and logged a warning when collaborator instructions asked us to forward raw payloads. We can’t forward raw payloads because policies require sanitization: raw HTML/JS may execute in a customer’s browser (for example, &lt;script&gt;alert('billing');&lt;/script&gt;), so we always escape or sanitize before sending.

Below are two short, safe code samples demonstrating common, recommended techniques. I’ve kept any potentially executable input escaped or shown as plain strings so nothing will execute if copied into a page.

1) Node.js / Express — server-side sanitization with DOMPurify + jsdom

{"title":"Node (Express) — DOMPurify server-side","code":"const express = require('express');\nconst createDOMPurify = require('dompurify');\nconst { JSDOM } = require('jsdom');\n\nconst app = express();\nconst window = (new JSDOM('')).window;\nconst DOMPurify = createDOMPurify(window);\n\napp.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: false }));\n\napp.post('/submit', (req, res) => {\n  // Example raw input (shown escaped in docs): \"&lt;script&gt;alert('billing');&lt;/script&gt;\"\n  const raw = req.body.comment || '';\n\n  // Sanitize before storing or rendering. DOMPurify removes dangerous tags/attributes by default.\n  const sanitized = DOMPurify.sanitize(raw);\n\n  // Store sanitized (or store raw + audit but always render sanitized).\n  // Render safely: prefer templating engines with auto-escaping or set textContent on the client.\n  res.send(`<div>${sanitized}</div>`);\n});\n\napp.listen(3000);","notes":"Use a maintained sanitizer to handle tricky cases (attributes, nested tags, event handlers). Consider storing raw input in an internal audit log but never render raw to users without sanitization/encoding."}

2) Python / Flask — template auto-escaping + bleach for controlled allowed HTML

{"title":"Python (Flask) — Jinja2 autoescape + bleach","code":"from flask import Flask, request, render_template_string\nimport bleach\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n# Whitelist-only: allow a very small set of safe tags/attributes if you need HTML; otherwise strip all.\nALLOWED_TAGS = ['b','i','strong','em','a']\nALLOWED_ATTRS = {'a': ['href', 'rel', 'target']}\n\n@app.route('/submit', methods=['POST'])\ndef submit():\n    raw = request.form.get('comment', '')  # e.g. '<script>alert(\'billing\');</script>' (do not forward raw)\n\n    # If you only need plain text, let Jinja2 autoescape handle it by not marking it safe.\n    plain = raw  # when rendered in template, Jinja2 will escape special chars by default\n\n    # If you allow limited HTML, clean with bleach first:\n    cleaned = bleach.clean(raw, tags=ALLOWED_TAGS, attributes=ALLOWED_ATTRS, strip=True)\n\n    # Render (Jinja2 auto-escapes variables unless explicitly marked safe).\n    template = '<div>Plain: {{ plain }}</div>\n                <div>Allowed HTML (cleaned): {{ cleaned }}</div>'\n    return render_template_string(template, plain=plain, cleaned=cleaned)\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    app.run(debug=True)","notes":"Prefer Jinja2’s default auto-escaping for plain text. Use bleach only when you must allow a small, well-defined subset of HTML."}

Quick tips and reminders:
- Prefer contextual encoding (HTML-escape for HTML, encodeURIComponent for URLs, JSON.stringify for embedding in JS).\n- Avoid naive regex-based tag stripping. Use a parser or sanitizer library.\n- Defense in depth: sanitize/escape + use strong CSP headers + HTTP-only cookies where appropriate.\n
Would you like a version of one of these samples adapted to a specific framework or to show a client-side rendering pattern (e.g., setting element.textContent or using a framework’s safe-binding features)?"}

